## Proof of Erdős Problem 459

**Setup.** For $u \in \mathbb{N}$, define
$$S(u) = \{v \in \mathbb{N} : v > u \;\text{and}\; \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(v) \subseteq \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u)\},$$
so that $f(u) = \min S(u)$ (the infimum is attained since $S(u)$ is a nonempty set of natural numbers, as shown below).

---

### Theorem 1 (`erdos_459`): For every $u \ge 2$, $\;u + 2 \le f(u) \le u^2$.

**Lower bound: $f(u) \ge u + 2$.**

We show $u+1 \notin S(u)$, which forces every element of $S(u)$ to be $\ge u+2$ (since all elements are $> u$, hence $\ge u+1$, and $u+1$ is excluded).

Since $\gcd(u,\, u+1) = 1$, no prime divisor of $u+1$ can divide $u$. Because $u \ge 2$, we have $u+1 \ge 3 \ge 2$, so $u+1$ possesses at least one prime factor $p$. This $p$ satisfies $p \mid (u+1)$ and $p \nmid u$, hence
$$p \in \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u+1) \setminus \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u).$$
Therefore $\mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u+1) \not\subseteq \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u)$, so $u+1 \notin S(u)$. It follows that $\min S(u) \ge u+2$, i.e., $f(u) \ge u+2$.

**Upper bound: $f(u) \le u^2$.**

We exhibit $u^2 \in S(u)$. First, $u \ge 2$ gives $u^2 - u = u(u-1) \ge 2 \cdot 1 = 2 > 0$, so $u^2 > u$. Second, squaring does not introduce or remove any prime factors:
$$\mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u^2) = \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u) \subseteq \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(u).$$
Hence $u^2 \in S(u)$, so $S(u) \neq \emptyset$ and $f(u) = \min S(u) \le u^2$.

Combining both parts: $\boxed{u + 2 \le f(u) \le u^2}$ for all $u \ge 2$. $\quad\blacksquare$

---

### Theorem 2 (`erdos_459.variants.upper_tight`): If $p$ is prime, then $f(p) = p^2$.

Since $p$ is prime, $\mathrm{PrimeFactors}(p) = \{p\}$. Thus
$$S(p) = \{v > p : \mathrm{PrimeFactors}(v) \subseteq \{p\}\}.$$
An integer whose only prime factor is $p$ is exactly a power $p^k$ ($k \ge 1$). The constraint $v > p$ becomes $p^k > p$, i.e., $k \ge 2$. The elements of $S(p)$ are therefore $\{p^2, p^3, p^4, \dots\}$, and the minimum is
$$f(p) = \min S(p) = p^2. \quad\blacksquare$$

